raw:
{
  "slots": {
    "question": [
      {
        "claim": "Does the relationship between a person’s political conservatism and COVID-19 vaccine hesitancy or uptake depend on the political climate where that person lives?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The goal of the present research was to examine how political climate moderates\nthe relationship between self-reported political conservatism and COVID-19 vaccine\nhesitancy and uptake.",
            "page": 1
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Does the pattern differ between attitudes and reported behavior, or between initial vaccination and boosting?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Additionally, by looking at both COVID vaccine and booster status and hesitancy, we\nidentify how attitudes and behaviors may have shifted as we transitioned from initial vaccine doses to boosters.",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "central_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "Regional politics mattered more for conservatives than for liberals: living in a more liberal area was associated with less hesitancy and a greater likelihood of boosting among conservatives. This interaction did not extend to receipt of the initial vaccine.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "our findings indicate that political climate, as represented through regional 2020 presidential election votes,\nhas a stronger association with vaccine and booster hesitancy for individuals who are higher on political conservatism as\ncompared to those who are politically liberal.",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "Liberals\nwere more likely to receive a booster regardless of political climate, while conservatives’ likelihood was associated with their political climate.",
            "page": 1
          },
          {
            "text": "The interaction for vaccination was not significant",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors argue that this geographic variation among conservatives is relevant to deciding whom to reach with annual vaccination efforts.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Observed patterns linking\nliberal political climates with vaccine uptake among conservative individuals have\nimportant implications for vaccination efforts among conservative individuals in the\nUnited States.",
            "page": 1
          },
          {
            "text": "These results have significant\nimplications for populations to target to increase COVID-19 vaccine uptake as we move towards annual vaccinations.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "evidence": [
      {
        "claim": "The data came from a U.S. convenience sample recruited through Qualtrics Panels for an online self-report survey conducted from March 8 to April 19, 2023.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "This was an online self-reported study in which a nonprobability convenience sample\nwas recruited from the United States between March 8 and April 19, 2023.",
            "page": 2
          },
          {
            "text": "Participants were a convenience sample recruited through Qualtrics Panels, an online research platform that manages\nparticipant recruitment and data quality for academic studies.",
            "page": 3
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors reanalyzed data originally collected to study marginalization. Recruitment crossed Black or White racial identity with income above or below the U.S. median and sought to limit gender imbalance within each quota.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The present research presents a novel analysis of the\ndataset from this project, focusing on political orientation and political climate, rather than marginalization.",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "Half of the sample was requested to be White (and no other racial identity), and half the sample was requested\nto be Black (and no other racial identity). Within each racial group, half of the participants were requested to be below\nthe median annual income in the US [$70,000 [28]].",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "Additionally, within each quota group it was requested that no more than 60% of the\nparticipants be of any gender.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Of 1,777 respondents, 798 remained after response-quality checks. Excluding those without a usable ZIP code or matching election data left 683 participants.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Qualtrics Panels collected data from 1777 participants, and identified 970 participants as not passing all quality screens\n(described above). We eliminated an additional nine participants whose open-ended responses did not make logical\nsense, resulting in a sample of 798 validated participants.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "We eliminated an additional 115 participants who did not provide valid zip code data (N = 100) or whose zip code did not have corresponding election data (N = 15). The final sample\nfor these analyses included 683 participants.",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The sample spanned ages 18–94 and areas with Republican vote shares ranging from about 7% to 89%. Initial vaccination was more common than boosting: 76% reported the former and 53% the latter.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Participants ranged in age from 18 to 94 years (M = 46.29, SD = 18.94).",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "The % Republican vote by zip code\nranged from 6.53% to 88.63% (M = 45.08%, SD = 18.09%).",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "519 (76%) of participants reported receiving the initial COVID-19 vaccine and 365 (53%) reported\nreceiving at least one COVID-19 booster.",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Individual conservatism was the average of two self-ratings, one on social issues and one on economic issues, on scales running from 0 (strongly liberal) to 10 (strongly conservative).",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "“social issues (for example, abortion, gun rights, gay rights)”.",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "“economic issues (for example, taxation, government spending)”. Both items were rated on a 11-point sliding scale from\nstrongly liberal (0) to strongly conservative (10). These two items were averaged to form a political conservatism scale.",
            "page": 3
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The contextual predictor was Republican vote share in the 2020 presidential election, derived by matching respondents’ ZIP codes to election reporting areas and averaging when a ZIP code matched multiple areas.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We assessed regional political climate using zip code data provided\nby participants and 2020 presidential election results sourced from the New York Times [22].",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "Political climate for each participant was determined by matching FIPS codes to corresponding zip\ncodes. In cases where a zip code included multiple FIPS codes, data was averaged across these FIPS.",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "We used this\n% Republican vote by zip code as a measure of conservative regional climate.",
            "page": 3
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Two separate survey questions recorded whether a participant had received a COVID-19 vaccine and whether they had received a booster, defined as a dose after the initial series.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Self-reported vaccine status and booster status were assessed through individual items, “have you received a COVID19 vaccine” and “have you received a COVID-19 booster”. The term “booster” is used in this analysis as this was the\ncommon term at the time to refer to subsequent doses of the COVID-19 vaccine after the initial vaccine series.",
            "page": 3
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The researchers adapted seven adult-vaccination attitude items to measure COVID-19 vaccine hesitancy and used items from a separate 2022 survey to measure booster hesitancy.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Vaccine hesitancy (i.e., negative attitudes toward vaccination) was measured\nusing the seven items from the Attitudes towards Adult Vaccination Scale [24], which assesses the perceived value of\nvaccines in general. We modified the items to assess attitudes specifically toward the COVID-19 vaccine",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "Booster hesitancy was\nassessed using items from a 2022 survey on COVID-19 booster hesitancy [25].",
            "page": 3
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors examined the hesitancy measures with confirmatory factor analysis and internal-consistency checks. Both scales had a Cronbach’s alpha of .93, and item responses were averaged within each scale.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We performed confirmatory factor analysis on the items for the vaccine and booster hesitancy scales (S2 File) and the\nsubsequent reliability of the scales was calculated using Cronbach’s alphas.",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "The vaccine hesitancy items demonstrated adequate reliability (α = .93, 95% CI [.92,.93]) and were\naveraged to form the vaccine hesitancy scale. The booster hesitancy items demonstrated adequate reliability (α = .93, 95%\nCI [.93,.94]) and were averaged to form the booster hesitancy scale.",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Before modeling the interaction, the authors examined pairwise correlations and compared groups using t-tests and chi-square tests.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Pearson correlations were used to assess\ncorrelations between all non-nominal variables",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "differences in\nfrequencies of vaccine status and booster status by gender and race were assessed using X2. Independent samples\nt-tests were used to assess differences in all non-nominal variables across vaccinated and unvaccinated participants.\nThe same analyses were run to assess differences between boosted and unboosted participants.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The moderation tests used linear regression for hesitancy scores and logistic regression for uptake. Models added the two political predictors and then their interaction to controls for age, gender, education, income, race, and perceived vaccine access; respondents with missing model data were excluded.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Multiple linear regression models were built to assess the association between political conservatism and conservative regional climate with\nvaccine hesitancy, controlling for age, gender, education, income, race, and perceived vaccine access.",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "Model 2 included control variables and political conservatism and conservative regional climate. Model 3 included all variables from Model 2 and the interaction between\npolitical conservatism and conservative regional climate.",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "same process was used with booster hesitancy as the outcome variable",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "Additionally, we ran two sets of generalized linear models with logit link\nand model-­based standard errors predicting vaccine status and booster status from the same three models described\nabove.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "For all analyses, any participants with missing data on any of these variables were excluded from the analysis.",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "key_findings": [
      {
        "claim": "Personal conservatism and a more conservative regional climate each predicted greater hesitancy about both kinds of dose and lower reported uptake of both, after adjustment.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Political conservatism and conservative regional climate were both associated with higher levels of COVID-19 vaccine and booster hesitancy and lower rates of self-reported vaccination and boosting, when controlling for age, education, income, and race.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Initial-vaccine hesitancy was low among liberals across regions. The increase in hesitancy with personal conservatism was steeper in more conservative areas.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The interaction for vaccine hesitancy indicates that at the lowest levels of conservatism (i.e., liberalism) vaccine hesitancy is lower regardless of regional climate.\nAs political conservatism increases vaccine hesitancy also increases, but this increase is even greater for individuals\nwho live in a more conservative regional climate.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Conservatives were more hesitant about boosters in conservative areas than in liberal ones. Among the most liberal respondents, the small regional difference ran in the opposite direction.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "There was a similar pattern for booster hesitancy, with more politically\nconservative individuals in a conservative regional climate having higher booster hesitancy than conservative individuals\nin a liberal regional climate. For the most politically liberal individuals, booster hesitancy was slightly lower in more conservative regional climates.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The estimated probability of boosting exceeded 65% for the most liberal respondents across climates. For the most conservative, it was about 60% in liberal areas but below 35% in conservative ones.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "the most liberal participants were over 65% likely to get boosted regardless of climate. The most conservative participants in liberal climates were 60% likely to get boosted, while conservatives in conservative climates were less\nthan 35% likely to get boosted (OR =.83, p = .026).",
            "page": 8
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Unlike booster receipt, initial vaccine receipt showed no detectable interaction between personal ideology and regional politics (OR 1.02, p = .833).",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The interaction between political conservatism\nand conservative regional climate (Table 6) was not significant (OR = 1.02, p = .833).",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "scope_and_limits": [
      {
        "claim": "ZIP-code vote share is a proxy for political climate. Other local characteristics correlated with voting, such as population density, could account for the associations.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Because we used 2020 presidential election votes by zip code as a proxy for political climate, it must be noted that\nother zip code-based differences could be driving the associations noted in this paper. Regional factors (e.g., population\ndensity) may play a role in linking 2020 election votes to vaccine attitudes and behaviors.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Adjusting for demographic characteristics did not establish that the associations were the same in every subgroup. Pooled estimates could conceal different subgroup patterns.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "While our analyses controlled\nfor demographic covariates commonly associated with vaccine hesitancy, we did not formally assess effect modification\nby these factors.",
            "page": 10
          },
          {
            "text": "Consequently, the reported pooled associations should be interpreted as average effects across the\nsample, and we cannot exclude the possibility that heterogeneity across demographic subgroups could produce aggregation effects such as Simpson’s paradox.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Reported vaccination cannot be equated with verified vaccination because memory errors and socially desirable answers may affect self-report.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Self-report is susceptible to social desirability [30] and recall bias [31], and we cannot assume that reported vaccine\nstatus reflects actual vaccine status.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Online recruitment excluded people without access to the survey platform.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Additionally, our sample was recruited through an online platform and only includes\nparticipants with access to this platform.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The Black and White panel sample may not represent the wider U.S. population.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Further, our sample includes only White and Black participants recruited through\nQualtrics Panels, and may not generalize to broader populations in the United States.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The cross-sectional measurements do not establish causation or the direction of the associations.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Finally, it should be noted that this\ndata is cross-sectional and we cannot assume causal or directional associations.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The study did not test why booster decisions varied with political climate. Social pressure is a possible explanation, and the authors leave open whether the asymmetry reflects a feature of liberal environments or greater responsiveness to context among conservatives.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "While we did not asses the mechanism through which this occurs, these\nfindings suggest that environmental factors (e.g., social pressure) may impact decisions to receive annual COVID-19\ndoses.",
            "page": 10
          },
          {
            "text": "The interaction between political climate and\norientation suggests that liberal environments may exert more influence on conservative individuals than conservative\nenvironments exert on liberal individuals. It could also be the case that conservative individuals are more influenced by\ntheir environments than their liberal counterparts.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "theoretical_positioning": [
      {
        "claim": "The authors extend two strands of research on the politics of vaccination: studies of individual ideology and studies of geographic political context. They combine the two by treating context as a moderator rather than only as a separate predictor.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "While previous research has identified the unique impact\nof both political orientation and political climate on vaccination attitudes, there has\nbeen little research on the interaction between personal political orientation and local\npolitical climate.",
            "page": 2
          },
          {
            "text": "The present research examines political climate as a potential moderator of the\nassociation between political orientation and COVID-19 vaccine hesitancy and\nuptake.",
            "page": 2
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "interlocutor_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "Rönn, Menzies, and Salomon found that counties and states with a higher Democratic vote share in 2020 had higher subsequent COVID-19 vaccination rates. The authors build on this geographic association by asking whether it varies with residents’ own ideology.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "For example, at both the county and the state level there was\na positive association between Democratic vote share (i.e., % of people voting for the\nDemocratic candidate) in the 2020 presidential election and COVID-19 vaccination\nrates from 2021−2022 [19].",
            "page": 2
          },
          {
            "text": "Here, we examined the moderating role of political climate on the association\nbetween individual political orientation and COVID-19 vaccine hesitancy.",
            "page": 2
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
